body:
Inside a `repeat`, every copy of a `stack` lands where the last copy goes, even when the stack's `at` uses the repeat's index or a `group` around it does. A `box` or a `group` without a stack in it gets the right place in each copy.

```acre
canvas {
    size auto auto;
    let c #ff0000 #00ff00 #0000ff;
    repeat v 0 2 { stack { at (v * 50) 0; box { size 40 40; fill (c v); } } }
    repeat v 0 2 { group { at (v * 50) 60; stack { box { size 40 40; fill (c v); } } } }
    repeat v 0 2 { box { at (v * 50) 120; size 40 40; fill (c v); } }
}
```

The first two rows draw one blue square each, at the third copy's place. The third row draws red, green and blue side by side.

`figures/decade-palette.acre` draws three columns with one `repeat`. Each column had to give up `stack` and place every section by hand with `at`.
